    "description": "3 Piece Vintage Art Pottery Lot 1942 Roseville Peony Candlestick & Matching Pair of 1940s Hull Wildrose Cornucopia Candlesticks\n·        Add a stunning collection of authentic 1940s American art pottery to your home with this beautiful 3-piece estate lot. This curated set includes an iconic Roseville double candlestick holder and a perfectly matching pair of Hull miniature cornucopia vases. Together, they create an exceptional mid-century display for a fireplace mantel, dining table centerpiece, or holiday console arrangement.\n·        Includes:\no  One Roseville Pottery Peony Double Candlestick Holder (Shape 1153). Introduced in 1942 by the famous Zanesville, Ohio pottery company. This highly collectible piece features a textured green base that blends beautifully into warm coral and yellow hues. It is decorated on both sides with large, crisply embossed peony blossoms. The bottom shows the authentic, raised Roseville U.S.A. 1153 cursive script signature. It measures approximately 4.5 inches tall by 7.5 inches long.\n·        Two Matching Pair of Hull Art Pottery Cornucopia Candlesticks (Shape 512). Produced in the mid-1940s by Hull Art Pottery in Crooksville, Ohio, as part of their beloved Wildrose and Dogwood line. This matching pair features a lovely matte gradient glaze that transitions smoothly from soft pastel pink down to a slate blue base. Each Candlestick is accented with delicate embossed wildrose clusters and finished with a unique scrolled handle base. Both pieces feature the authentic raised vertical HULL U.S.A. 512 block lettering on the inside of the unglazed bottom rim. They measure approximately 4 inches tall.\n·        All three pieces are in excellent vintage condition with no structural chips, cracks, or repairs. The Roseville candlestick displays beautiful, uniform glaze crazing across the yellow sections, which is an expected aging trait that confirms its 1940s authenticity. The Hull cornucopias are remarkably clean with no flea bites along their delicate scalloped rims or bases, and their pastel colors are beautifully matched.",
